Planned approach (summary)
- The bug arises because Point.vel() only returns a stored velocity (self._vel_dict[frame]) and otherwise raises a ValueError. But positions are stored (via set_pos) and it is possible to compute a point's velocity as the time derivative of its position vector (pos_from(...).dt(frame)). ReferenceFrame.ang_vel_in already computes angular velocity by following orientation data; Point.vel should analogously try to compute velocity from position relations when possible.
- I inspected Point and ReferenceFrame implementations and found two places to change:
  1. Point.vel — implement logic to compute velocity from position chains if a stored velocity isn't present.
  2. Point.acc — currently tries to access self._vel_dict[frame] directly in a way that will KeyError; it should use Point.vel so acceleration can be computed from dynamically computed velocities.

Files inspected
- sympy/physics/vector/point.py — full file read. Relevant methods: __init__, _pdict_list, pos_from, set_pos, set_vel, vel, acc.
- sympy/physics/vector/frame.py — to see how ReferenceFrame.ang_vel_in derives angular velocity from orientation chains and variable/time-differentiation patterns (used as a conceptual model).

Root cause
- Point.vel only checks for an existing stored entry in _vel_dict and raises ValueError if none is present:
  - This prevents deriving velocity from stored position vectors (pos relations) even when that derivation is straightforward using the vector time derivative (Vector.dt(frame)).
- The Point._pdict_list docstring even says the velocity and acceleration search may not work because _vel_dict keys are ReferenceFrame objects. The code never attempted to compute velocity from position relations. Meanwhile ReferenceFrame.ang_vel_in builds a chain and sums angular velocities derived from orientation (or via time-differentiation).
- Additionally, Point.acc uses self._vel_dict[frame] directly (without checking membership) which would raise KeyError. It also doesn't leverage vel() to compute a derivative if no acceleration is explicitly stored.

Complete fix — changes necessary
1) Modify Point.vel to attempt to compute a velocity from position relations when a stored velocity for the requested frame is not present. The behavior should be:
   - If self._vel_dict contains the velocity for the requested frame, return it (no change).
   - Otherwise, attempt to compute the velocity by traversing the graph of points connected by set_pos relations:
     - If the point has no connected position relations, raise the same ValueError as before.
     - Otherwise, recursively try to find a connected point that has a velocity in the requested frame; when a connected point with a known velocity is found, sum that velocity and the time derivative (in the requested frame) of the vector distance along the path. This recursion correctly sums derivatives along a chain of pos relations and yields the correct velocity.
     - If none of the connected points have a known velocity in the requested frame, return the time derivative (in the requested frame) of the position vector relative to some connected point (i.e., pos_from(neighbor).dt(frame)). This matches the user's expected behavior in the report (Q.vel(A) returning r.dt(A) even if P has no velocity set).
   - Prevent infinite recursion by tracking visited points.

   Rationale: For a chain P0 - r01 -> P1 - r12 -> P2 - ... - Pn, if Pn has vel in frame F, the velocity of P0 in F is vel(Pn,F) + d/dt_F(r_n...0) which equals summing dt of each link plus the anchor velocity. If none of the chain points have explicit velocity in F, returning the derivative of the relative vector matches what users expect when they only set positions that are functions of time.

2) Modify Point.acc to use Point.vel for computing acceleration when acceleration is not present:
   - If an acceleration is explicitly stored in _acc_dict for the requested frame, return it.
   - Otherwise, try to obtain the velocity via self.vel(frame). If velocity can be obtained, return its derivative dt(frame) (unless the velocity is zero).
   - If velocity cannot be determined (vel raises ValueError), return Vector(0) — this mirrors the original intent (when velocity is zero or not set, acceleration defaulted to Vector(0)). (Note: the original code attempted to access self._vel_dict[frame] directly and would KeyError; the proposed change is safer and consistent with dynamic computation.)

